      hypothesis: "Post-carve-out consumer companies systematically underestimate SC systems rebuild complexity and timeline. TSA extensions are costly (parent charges premium) but the alternative—rushing ERP and SC system independence—creates operational risk. The 18-24 month post-TSA period is the highest SC risk window for carved-out companies. External consulting support during this phase has the highest ROI of any engagement type.",
      evidence: "Ekaterra: Unilever SAP TSA expiry estimated 2024-25. Blending operations in UK and Netherlands still on transitional systems. Kenvue: J&J SAP TSA disclosed in IPO prospectus—expiry 2025. Procurement teams partially still using J&J contracts. Historical: Kraft post-Mondelez carve-out (2012) took 36 months to full SC independence. Reckitt Benckiser carve-out of Indivior (2014): 18-month TSA, 6-month extension required.",
      contradictions: "Budget vs. complexity mismatch: Ekaterra's SC transformation was originally budgeted at €120M with 24-month timeline. Actual cost now €180-220M with 36+ month timeline—a 50-80% overrun that follows historical carve-out patterns (Kraft-Mondelez, Reckitt-Indivior both overran 40-60%). PE return model built on original budget is now materially impaired. EBITDA improvement narrative vs. TSA cost drag: CVC Capital's thesis was post-Unilever cost rationalisation improving EBITDA 200-300bps. TSA extension costs are consuming the EBITDA headroom before the standalone savings materialise—the value creation timeline is compressed.",
      clientImplication: "PE sponsors and management teams at carved-out companies need external SC expertise for: (1) TSA exit planning—prioritising which systems to replace first, (2) standalone procurement contract negotiation, (3) interim SC management support during transition, (4) target operating model design for lean PE-owned SC.",
      thoucentriqAngle: "Post-Carve-Out SC Readiness Assessment: TSA dependency mapping, standalone system selection and implementation roadmap, procurement contract novation support, interim SC management. Typically 12-18 month engagement with PE sponsor as co-client.",
      kpiLinkage: "SC Technology Maturity Score — Ekaterra at 2.8 (carve-out systems transition); Procurement Savings Delivered — Post-carve-out procurement immaturity means 1.2% savings vs. 2.4% Unilever benchmark; Cash Conversion Cycle — TSA overhead consuming EBITDA, delaying PE exit model",
      signalCluster: "Ekaterra / Lipton: SAP Go-Live Delayed; Unilever Ice Cream Separation: Standalone Entity Operational from Q1 FY26",
      trajectoryContext: "FY22 (Past): Ekaterra and Kenvue carved out with full parent SC infrastructure support. TSA cushion made Day 1 operations viable. Original plan: 24-month systems independence, €120M budget. → FY24-25 (Current): Both carve-outs behind plan. Ekaterra: 36+ month timeline, €180-220M cost. Kenvue: ERP independence phase 2 in progress. A third carve-out (Unilever Ice Cream) now adds to global carve-out consulting demand. → Direction: TSA final exit is the highest-risk 12-month window—probability of operational disruption peaks here. → Inflection: Q3-Q4 FY26 is Ekaterra's SAP go-live attempt. Success or failure determines whether CVC Capital can initiate exit preparation in FY27.",
      financialImpact: "TSA extension cost: typically 20-40% premium on parent's internal cost allocation. For Ekaterra at ~€2.8B revenue: TSA cost excess vs. standalone = estimated €30-80M annually. Post-carve-out SC transformation investment: €50-100M over 3 years for a company of Ekaterra's scale. ROI: lower operating cost + EBITDA expansion for PE exit.",
      whyNow: "TSAs from Unilever and J&J are expiring NOW. PE sponsor (CVC for Ekaterra) is on a 5-7 year exit timeline—FY25-26 is the window to build SC capability before exit preparation starts. Companies in year 2-3 post-carve-out are in the highest-risk and highest-need phase for external SC consulting support.",
      relatedCompanies: "Ekaterra / Lipton,Kenvue India,Twinings",
      pitchAnchor: "Post-carve-out SC rebuilds are the most complex and highest-stakes consulting engagements in consumer goods—and Ekaterra and Kenvue are both in that window simultaneously. We have a proven TSA exit playbook from three prior carve-out programmes that cuts the risk of TSA extension cost and operational disruption.",
      provocQuestion: "When your TSA expires, which three SC capabilities are you most dependent on from the parent company—and which of those do you have no plan to replace independently?",
      version: "1.0",
      nextReview: "2025-07-01",
